  THE GATES, and the thresholds they are scored at:
    V0 all cells, >= 3 usable treads and >= 3 repeats per arm
    V1 one tread set, one BLOCK_M, one token count per tread
    V2 all 5 parts of the buffer proof: addresses, sentinels, kernel_read, shared_blind, same_layer
    V3 weight allocation within 1% of prediction; high-water mark under the plan's ceiling
    V4 every fitted tread of shared and private under 95% of the fixed roof
    V5 the far edge of b's 90% band, b the step-aware native - shared per-tile cost, < 3% of slope(private): the wider declaration's own per-M-tile cost, which ties the matched ratio to the study's call
    V6 shared and private within 2.0% at n=1, where they are the same call
    V7 shared and private under-load clocks within 1% at every tread; at FULL duty a card that cannot lock its clock fails this by construction whenever the arms draw different power, and below it this holds only at a duty low enough that the clock stops following power (session 4's clock arm: flat at 0.25, still tracking power at 0.5); --clock-elasticity PRINTS a corrected ratio beside the raw one, scored by nothing
    V8 the probed alignment steps in SHARED's and PRIVATE's id sets are worth <= 0.01 of the ratio together; FAIL needs it over budget AND resolved, and a FAIL alone skips the sweep; the probe is timed under a CUDA graph (16 calls per replay) so its cells are GPU time and NATIVE's switch beside them confirms or bounds, never withholds; an EAGER fallback that comes back host-bound is scored only if it resolves NATIVE's switch at the census tread
    C1 PASS: point and whole interval inside ALPHA_BAND; FAIL: interval misses ALPHA_BAND; otherwise UNKNOWN
    C2 the delivered weight-read rate is at or under the card's own, +10%

  THE LAYOUT, REGISTERED: copy c of expert e is slot e x 9 + c, and shared and private declare all E x 9 = 72 slots at every tread, so the two share the sorted-id buffer, the launch grid, the dead launches and the tile order, and differ ONLY in the addresses their tiles read. Of those 9 copies the deepest tread READS 6; the rest are padding, filled and never read, and the buffer proof zeroes each of them and requires neither arm's output to move.
  THE CAVEAT, REGISTERED: those addresses are also the TLB footprint and the DRAM page locality, so slope(private) is a BOUNDED PROXY for the no-reuse case and not the no-reuse case itself. V5 measures the declaration at depth, V6 this arm's own floor at n=1, V7 the clock. None is an argument; all are numbers on this page after the run.
  AND WHAT NONE BOUNDS: a cost paid by READING the copies that vanishes at one copy, which is the shape of a TLB or DRAM-page locality cost. NATIVE does not read them and n=1 has one. The only trace such a cost leaves here is the private ladder's own mean relative residual, printed in the ladder table and SCORED BY NOTHING.
  THE ALIGNMENT KERNEL, SEPARATED THREE WAYS: vLLM switches moe_align_block_size kernel at the cited id and expert bounds, and for this ladder the id bound falls inside it (native switches at tread 4). The ratio arms are declared past the expert bound so both take one kernel throughout (the census above, refused otherwise); the probe times the alignment op alone along the ladder once per ARM -- NATIVE's declaration, and SHARED's and PRIVATE's id sets at the ratio arms' -- and V8 scores both ratio series; and NATIVE, which keeps the study's declaration and its switch, has its difference from SHARED fitted with a step term so V5 reads the declaration's per-tile cost with the step out and the step is printed with an interval.
  AND TWO SMALL ASYMMETRIES, REGISTERED AND NOT REMOVED: on the scan kernel, `count_and_sort_expert_tokens` does one atomic add per id into a per-expert counter, so SHARED's 8 counters take 32 n increments each while PRIVATE's 8 x n take 32 each; a per-tread term in the numerator alone. The probe times the alignment op on PRIVATE's id set as well as SHARED's, and V8 prints slope(private ids) - slope(shared ids) in us per tread and in the ratio's units: MEASURED, printed, not scored. And the wider declaration puts the top expert slot 71 at 7.77 Gi ELEMENTS from the weight base in w1, past the 2^31 an int32 offset holds, which the kernel addresses through an int64 cast of `off_experts`; NATIVE's strided view multiplies its own index by the same stride and depends on the cast too. A build without it reads the wrong bytes, and V2's same_layer and kernel_read parts are what catch that.
  AND THE ASSUMPTION THE RATIO KEEPS: no rate enters the number, but the ratio is alpha as a traffic fraction only if both arms deliver the same bytes per second. The shared arm's non-re-read bytes come from L2, which is not free.
  NOT A READOUT: the LEVEL of any ladder. Only the slopes are compared; native's intercept differs from shared's by the declaration's dead launches, which are a constant per tread.
  NOT MEASURED HERE: alpha_a, the activation-side miss fraction. Both ladders carry the same activation term and the ratio subtracts nothing.
